Implement `SD_TestClockSweep` in `sd_test.c`. Add at the end:
```c
int SD_TestClockSweep(uint32_t start_lba, uint32_t block_count, SD_ClockResult *best) {
  static const uint32_t divs[] = {64U,32U,16U,8U};
  SD_ClockResult best_result = {0};
  uint32_t best_div = 64U;
  int found=0;
  if (best) memset(best,0,sizeof(*best));
  for each div:
    if (!SD_Card_InitAtClock(div)) { LOG_Printf("SD:SWEEP div=%lu init=FAIL\r\n",...); continue; }
    SD_SpeedTestResult r;
    if (SD_TestReadWrite(start_lba, block_count, &r)) {
      LOG_Printf("SD:SWEEP div=%lu write=%lu read=%lu verify=OK\r\n",...);
      if (!found || r.write_kbs > best_result.write_kbs) { best_result...; best_div=div; found=1; }
    } else {
      LOG_Printf("SD:SWEEP div=%lu FAIL\r\n",...);
    }
  if (!found) {
    (void)SD_Card_InitAtClock(64U);
    LOG_Write("SD:SWEEP no reliable clock, fallback div=64\r\n");
    return 0;
  }
  (void)SD_Card_InitAtClock(best_div);
  if (best) *best = best_result;
  LOG_Printf("SD:SWEEP_BEST div=%lu write=%lu read=%lu\r\n", ...);
  return 1;
}
```
- `SD_Card_InitAtClock` logs `SD:CLOCK div=...` each time.
- `SD_TestReadWrite` logs a lot; okay.
- The sweep `block_count` should be small, e.g., 128 (64KB). The caller passes128.
- After `SD_Card_InitAtClock`, the card info/partition logs are not printed; fine.
- The best div is chosen by write_kbs. If read is faster but write slower, write is the user's focus; choose write.
- If a higher div fails after a write, `SD_Card_InitAtClock` for the next div de-inits/re-inits; good.
- The test writes at `start_lba` (LBA 2048) 64KB; okay.
- `SD_TestReadWrite` includes `SD_WaitReady` and retries.
- The sweep leaves the card initialized at best_div; subsequent code (if any) uses it. The `SD:CLOCK` log shows.
- The `best` struct may be uninitialized if no success; set zeros.

Add at end of `sd_test.c`.

