content.text:
Potential issue: If `HAL_SD_ReadBlocks_DMA` returns HAL_OK but `SD_WaitTransfer` returns0, `read_status` remains HAL_OK, then we set `read_status=HAL_OK` and break unconditionally? The code:
```
read_status = HAL_SD_ReadBlocks_DMA(...);
if (read_status == HAL_OK && SD_WaitTransfer(5000)) {
  read_status = HAL_OK;
  break;
}
```
If WaitTransfer fails, read_status is still HAL_OK, so the loop continues to the next retry (because the if body not taken). Good. But after the loop, if the last iteration's WaitTransfer failed, read_status is HAL_OK (from the last ReadBlocks_DMA call), so `if (read_status != HAL_OK)` is false and it proceeds to memcmp with invalid data! Need to set `read_status = HAL_ERROR` when WaitTransfer fails. Fix:
```
if (read_status == HAL_OK) {
  if (SD_WaitTransfer(5000)) { break; }
  read_status = HAL_ERROR;
}
```
Let's edit.

input.oldString:
read_status = HAL_SD_ReadBlocks_DMA(&hsd, (uint8_t *)verify, 0U, 1U);
if (read_status == HAL_OK && SD_WaitTransfer(5000U))
{
    read_status = HAL_OK;
    break;
}

input.newString:
read_status = HAL_SD_ReadBlocks_DMA(&hsd, (uint8_t *)verify, 0U, 1U);
if (read_status == HAL_OK)
{
    if (SD_WaitTransfer(5000U))
    {
        break;
    }
    read_status = HAL_ERROR;
}

