Now add error logging in diskio for read/write failures (limited), and retries.

Modify SD_Diskio_RawRead/Write:

```c
static uint32_t sd_disk_io_fail_logs;

static void SD_Diskio_LogFailure(char rw, uint32_t lba, uint32_t blocks)
{
    if (sd_disk_io_fail_logs < 8U)
    {
        ++sd_disk_io_fail_logs;
        LOG_Printf("SD:FS_IO_FAIL %c lba=%lu n=%lu err=%lu\r\n", rw, ...);
    }
}
```

Need log_uart.h include.

Also, before a transfer, ensure the card is ready:
```c
if (!SD_WaitReady(2000U)) return 0;   // might be too aggressive for every call
```
Doing SD_WaitReady (CMD13) before each read/write adds overhead but improves reliability. FatFs calls are 512B–64KB; one CMD13 per call is negligible. But SD_WaitReady polls with HAL_Delay(1) — could add ~1 ms per call. For performance of large files it's fine (only per call).

Actually HAL_SD_ReadBlocks_DMA internally waits for the card state? HAL checks hsd.State only. Let's add readiness check in the retry path only, not every call:

Simplest robust approach:
```c
static int SD_Diskio_Transfer(int is_write, ...) {
  for attempt in 0..2:
     if attempt>0: SD_WaitReady(1000); HAL_Delay(10);
     status = is_write ? HAL_SD_WriteBlocks_DMA : HAL_SD_ReadBlocks_DMA;
     if status == HAL_OK && SD_WaitTransfer(5000) return 1;
  log; return 0;
}
```

That handles transient errors. Let's rewrite those two functions to share a helper.

Also, a subtlety: retrying a partial write is safe for FAT (idempotent sector writes).

Let me rewrite sd_diskio.c RawRead/RawWrite.

