Wait, maybe the MBR write went to LBA 0 but the card write cache... Actually if the write succeeded and the read mismatched, the read may have been stale (card not committed). Then diskio read LBA 0 again and got... old MBR.

Hmm: the old MBR (pre-0xDA) had part1 = 0x0C at 2048 with 8388608 sectors and part2 = 0x0C at 8390656/7133184. Our FindPartition picks the LAST FAT entry → would be 8390656. But the log says lba=2048 sectors=8388608, meaning only ONE valid FAT entry was found. That suggests the MBR content in that sector is: entry1 (0x0C, 2048, 8388608) present, entry2 empty or invalid.

Hmm! What if the write actually wrote only part of the buffer, or the MBR we built has a problem? Let's look: our new code writes entries at 446 (0xDA) and 462 (0x0C). If the write partially succeeded... 

Wait, maybe the issue: `SD_MBR_FillEntry(&mbr_bytes[446U], 0x00U, 0xDAU, ...)` — the log didn't show part2 type. Let's check the verification logic: it compares mbr[] with verify[] using memcmp of the whole 512 bytes. If the card wrote only the first 512 bytes correctly, they'd match.

Since verify mismatched, the read got different data. What data? Probably the OLD MBR (2048/8388608 + ...). And then diskio read it and found entry1 = 0x0C (old type!) at 2048.

So the write did NOT take effect at all, yet HAL reported OK and SD_WaitTransfer OK.

